menu "LagunBus — env"

config LAGUN_TWAI_TX
    int "TWAI transmit GPIO"
    default 4
    help
        4 and 5 by default, because on the ESP32-C3 Super Mini they are broken out
        and play no other role. AVOID 2, 8 and 9 — those are boot-mode strapping
        pins, and their state at reset decides whether the board starts at all.
        Avoid 18 and 19 too: USB Serial/JTAG goes that way, that is, flashing and
        the log.

config LAGUN_TWAI_RX
    int "TWAI receive GPIO"
    default 5

config LAGUN_I2C_SDA
    int "I2C SDA GPIO (SHT40, VEML7700)"
    default 6
    help
        SDA of both sensors on one bus. 6 and 7 by default: broken out on the Super
        Mini, on the same side as the CAN RX, and no strapping role. Breakout modules
        usually carry their own pull-ups; without them 4.7 kOhm to 3V3 on each line.

config LAGUN_I2C_SCL
    int "I2C SCL GPIO (SHT40, VEML7700)"
    default 7

config LAGUN_SERVICE_GPIO
    int "Service button GPIO"
    default 9
    help
        The service button: held 5 s and let go, a factory reset by the device itself
        (devices/README.md). 9 by default — BOOT on the Super Mini, wired to ground; an
        external button goes from GPIO9 to GND next to it. Held at power-on it enters
        the download mode instead, which harms nothing: let go and power up again.

config LAGUN_STATUS_LED_GPIO
    int "Status LED GPIO"
    default 8
    help
        The status LED: what the node is doing (devices/README.md). 8 by default — the
        on-board LED of the Super Mini; an external one goes from 3V3 through a resistor
        to GPIO8. GPIO8 is a strapping pin and must not be pulled to ground at reset.

config LAGUN_STATUS_LED_ACTIVE_LOW
    bool "Status LED lights on a LOW level"
    default y
    help
        Yes for the Super Mini's on-board LED and for an external one wired from 3V3.
        Keep the VEML7700 away from it, or the sensor measures its own blinking.

config LAGUN_BITRATE
    int "Bus speed [bit/s]"
    default 500000
    help
        Has to agree with the hub and with EVERY other node. A node at a different
        speed not only hears nobody — it corrupts frames for all the others.

endmenu
